            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attack hatch



** Coming from the crawlspace opening into the basement, and the header in the main basement



** The older windows and doors throughout the house



** The current chimneys (oil boiler &amp; wood boiler) that each vent into the brick chimney



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I estimate your extensive renovation (full interior down to the studs and the exterior) will bring the air leakage of this house down to the target of a new home - 1.5 air changes per hour. This would represent a 71% reduction in air leakage heat loss, that is estimated to save $ 1,756 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 50.9 GJ, and qualify for a $ 1,000 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of this 1950 house appear to be 2x4 with R8 paper backed batt insulation. The bedroom addition at the back of the house appears to be the same construction, and the rear wall of that bedroom adjoins the garage.



You mentioned that the interior of this house will be gutted &amp; the insulation within the studs will be increased, as well as new insulation added to the exterior of the walls. The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP)  that was launched on April 1, 2019, can help you cover the cost of some additional insulation for the exterior wall renovation you are planning. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



For now, I have assumed the insulation between the studs will be replaced with R14, and another minimum 3" (R15) of extruded polystyrene rigid insulation will be added to the exterior, resulting in a net R21 nominal increase in the wall insulation. This will apply to 100% of the walls, including the wall that adjoins the bedroom to the garage.



Savings: By adding a minimum R21 to all of the exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 1,157 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 33.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic insulation includes the original R8 paper backed batt insulation &amp; there has been just about 2" (on average) of vermiculite insulation added on top - compressing some of the original batt insulation. I estimate the total current R value of the attic insulation to be no more than R12. In conjunction with your full renovation of this house, I have modeled the opportunity to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R50.



I emailed you on the afternoon of September 26 with some Health Canada information about vermiculite insulation and some NWT resources you can connect with for vermiculite testing and abatement.



Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R12 to R50, this is estimated to save $ 934 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 27.1 GJ, and qualify for a $ 1,500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilings>
                <foundation>

The basement below the original house &amp; the crawlspace below the rear bedroom addition are both uninsulated - nothing on the concrete walls, floor or in the header space above. In conjunction with your complete renovation of this house, I have modeled the following insulation upgrades:

- R24 added to the basement &amp; crawlspace walls, including the header space above

- R10 added to the floor of the basement &amp; crawlspace



As discussed, the floor joist ends (in the header space) are currently encased in mortar, and this may need to be removed before you insulate the header space, to prevent any moisture from wicking up from the foundation wall &amp; rotting out the ends of the floor joists. Please research this further with your contractor.



Savings: By adding a minimum R24 to the foundation walls &amp; header spaces above, and a minimum R10 to the floors, this is estimated to save $ 2,975 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 86.3 GJ, and qualify for a $ 3,000 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house (15 total between the basement and main floor) are older double pane wood windows, that have been well maintained over the years however are in desperate need of an upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 1,110 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 32.2 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window in rebates (12 windows maximum - so you are eligible for the maximum $3,000 rebate) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <doors>

Both of the exterior doors in this house are older solid wood doors with single pane glass windows. I recommend you upgrade both of these older doors to an ENERGY STAR?????? rated insulated doors for our climate zone.



Rebates:

By replacing these 2 older doors with ones that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified for our climate zone, you could potentially save $ 128 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.7 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for upgrading toilets.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>

This house currently has very little mechanical exhaust (just the clothes dryer) and nothing to ventilate the kitchen, bathroom or any other areas of the house. There is no fresh air supply to the house other than opening that high wall vent in the rear addition bedroom or opening a window. 



With your plans to complete a full renovation of this house (and your track record for building energy tight housing), I fully expect you will bring the air leakage of this house down to the target of a new home - 1.5 air changes per hour. This would represent a 71% reduction in air leakage from where the house currently is.



As you "Renovate Tight", you will also want to ensure you "Ventilate Right", which means you should plan to incorporate an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) while you have all of the walls, floors &amp; ceilings opened up. I would recommend a 75% efficient HRV.



Savings:

By installing a 75% efficient ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, this will actually increase the ventilation of this house (which is a good thing) and there is as estimated additional $ 126 per year in energy costs, and an increase to your annual energy usage by 2.6 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for installing an HRV. </VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

The domestic hot water supply for this house is provided for using a heating loop off the boiler. In conjunction with the boiler upgrade recommendation, the savings for the domestic hot water are included in that upgrade. Please look at the section of my report about the "Heating System".</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a Weil McLain WTGO-4 oil boiler, that it 85% AFUE and vents through a 7" flue connected into the side of the old brick chimney. There is also an older Fawcett B140 wood boiler that is interconnected to the boiler heat system and has its' own flue that is connected into the other side of the brick chimney. There is no combustion air supply for either of these heating appliances, so they draw all of their combustion air from inside the house. The oil boiler has a combi loop connected to the electric hot water tank to supplement the hot water during the heating season.



In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage), I have modeled an upgrade where you will eliminate both the oil &amp; wood boilers and install an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, and use this boiler to also provide the hot water for this house, incorporating a hot water storage tank.



Savings: By replacing the oil &amp; wood boilers with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, and using this boiler to also provide the hot water for this house, you could potentially save $ 3,648 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 84.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



Savings:

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the front facing roof of your house (orientation = SW), you could potentially save $ 929 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11.1 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program for electricity generation systems installed in a Hydro Community, including Yellowknife. 

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has an older 13 litre toilet that should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify you for the maximum $20,000 rebate from this program. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP that, together, will be capped at $20,000.



     - $8,000 for the wall insulation

     - $3,000 for the windows

     - $1,500 for the attic insulation

     - $3,000 for the foundation insulation

     - $1,500 for the heating system

     - $1,000 for the air leakage reduction

     - $4,000 for the bonus rebate



In addition to the $20,000 rebate from the DHERP, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ESTAR fridge &amp; washing machine - $200 each

     - ESTAR ventless (condensing) clothes dryer - $400

     - ECM Pumps (for your boiler &amp; the water circulation pump) - $175 each - 2 per fiscal year

     - Programmable Thermostats - 25% up to $175 each - 2 per fiscal year

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (just the clothes dryer, as the range hood recirculates inside the house and there is no bathroom fan), indicated a pressure drop of 1.1 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil boiler and wood boiler vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did not see any smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - a clothes dryer

        - a high wall passive vent in the rear addition bedroom

        - some operable windows



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



If you plan to install an HRV into this house (renovate tight .... ventilate right), than incorporate the bathroom exhaust through the HRV, with a boost switch. Please see the section of my report about a "Ventilation System".



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre delivered

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



